Adding a `PUSH` format means editing this method directly — there's no seam for new behavior to plug into without modifying existing, already-tested code.

```java
interface MessageFormatter { String format(String content); }

class FormatterRegistry {
    private final Map<String, MessageFormatter> formatters = new HashMap<>();
    void register(String type, MessageFormatter f) { formatters.put(type, f); }
    String format(String type, String content) {
        MessageFormatter f = formatters.get(type);
        if (f == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException(type);
        return f.format(content);
    }
}

// Adding PUSH later:
registry.register("PUSH", content -> content); // one new line, zero edits to FormatterRegistry
```

The dispatch code (`FormatterRegistry.format`) never changes again — new behavior is a new registration, not a new branch.
